00:00:00.799 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
i'm madison mills welcome back to  bloomberg quick take this week marks a  hundred days since the u.s withdrew its  military presence from afghanistan  following nearly 20 years of conflict in  the nation at the time a lot of  predictions were made following the fall  of kabul but more than three months on  how is the nation doing and where are we  at with those predictions here with the  status update from washington is dr  ariel cohen senior fellow at the  atlantic council dr cohen welcome back  to quick take and thanks for being here  i want to jump right in starting with  the big question on terrorism about  three months ago a lot of the guests we  had on this show talked about the  pending return of al qaeda where do we  stand with that today and what evidence  have you seen if any of a big return to  that level of terror  first of all  i just came back from the region i was  in kazakhstan this is not a border state  but it's still in the heart of eurasia  as is afghanistan and those  folks in kazakhstan keep a close uh and  weary eye on afghanistan  i can tell you that the big challenge  now  is the um clash  between taliban and uh isis uh josh uh  and um  uh sorry and um the threat  uh isis is presenting  to the taliban  so  while the taliban is already  trying to have contacts with  the u.s with qatar with pakistan isis is  in a full jihad mode is fighting um its  taliban uh posts as well as the rest of  the world so these two are duking it out  uh for the control of afghanistan and  many uh among the local  radicals are joining isis uh the second  issue is  that we have a lot of refugees leaving  afghanistan vast majority of them for  good reasons they don't see the future  uh there a lot of people are starving i  was trying desperately to help some  people to leave uh so far  unsuccessful it's very hard  but among these tens of thousands or  hundreds of thousands of refugees may be  uh radicals who are sent to the west to  uh establish sales uh to conduct attacks  or to conduct attacks in the future so  it's absolutely crucial that the  services in europe and in the united  states screen people who are knocking on  our doors and are saying that they're  refugees  from the pretty horrible regime of  course that taliban had established in  afghanistan and in kabul tell me about  what grade you would give the biden  administration in terms of predicting  how the us withdraw from the region  would impact uh relations between isis  and the taliban for example  uh the overall execution of that  withdrawal is an f  this  is  a collapse of u.s  power structure  in an area that was spent 20 years and  over a trillion dollars trying to  engage in what is called nation building  misleading ourselves telling ourselves  stories uh about what we can accomplish  or not accomplish in afghanistan what we  needed to do there and we failed to do  was go in destroy al qaeda uh destroy  the terrorist infrastructure and then  leave whoever was capable of ruling that  place and uh leave the minimal uh  footprint if we didn't do that if we  stayed there for so long we needed to  continue doing what we're doing in iraq  it's called status of forces agreement  we can leave 2500 3 000  select troops to coordinate airstrikes  to train to equip uh to collect  intelligence now we're blind and deaf  and mute we do not have eyes on the  ground a lot of conversations that i had  when i travel in central asia uh in july  and now is how  crippled we are by  leaving uh afghanistan in its totality  and not keeping an eye uh eyes and ears  on the ground well i wonder to what  extent though that might have been  inevitable i could imagine a biden  administration official saying that  clashes between leaders in the region  and isis were going to happen regardless  of whether or not you the u.s kept  troops on the ground  correct and uh i would point out that  the agreement for us pull out was  undertaken under the trump  administration  but biden continued biden was an  opponent of this broad engagement uh he  was  a supporter of anti-terrorism  approach not  anti-insurgency approach this is  washington speak for broad engagement  versus narrow engagement but once you  were discussing the modalities of that  disengagement or withdrawal you needed  to understand not just the deadline that  biden uh selected the 31st  of uh august you needed to understand  what happened next and we failed in  securing our allies we failed people who  trusted us with their lives we failed  women  who  uh did achieve quite a lot in  the modern post taliban afghanistan for  20 years and now some of them being  hunted and executed and raped and killed  it is horrible what is happening to the  more advanced uh more accomplished  afghan women there were police officers  diplomats um and professors whose lives  are destroyed today or  yeah killed uh in a brutal way in  afghanistan today and i hate to say it  we have something to do with that  spokesman from the taliban were very  quick after the fall of kabul to talk  about how the rights of women and girls  would be different this time around  right that education would be preserved  that freedom would be preserved as well  for women and girls  what have you seen in terms of those  promises did those completely fall flat  was any of it true  uh  from what i understand uh the  mixed classes mixed education is  finished uh i do believe that in the  majority of  careers or professions women were all  kicked out they were told not to come to  work uh  i think with the exception of hospitals  they they need those nurses uh possibly  doctors uh to take care of their sick  and wounded and in universities i  understand that uh women are limited to  women-only classes taught by women or  maybe in saudi like in saudi arabia they  had professors  remotely  teaching  via skype or via  zoom but not being present in the room  of course it limits uh the  um  the  ability of those women to interact they  were with their professors and teachers  and uh it also uh  they also don't allow women to travel  without a male  companion that also happened in saudi  arabia until recently  and in iran  so that is a part of sharia  rules for  women traveling in general people who  are trying to leave afghanistan are  encountering uh two barriers one  internal by the taliban and also  external when people when countries do  not provide them visas so this situation  is critical we need to do more to help  people who want to leave especially our  allies our assets  our  collaborators we need to get them out  the biden administration had also  promised that all individuals who had  assisted u.s troops on the ground would  get out if they wanted to get out and  then later suggested that some  individuals did choose to stay behind  where are we at with that  well  unfortunately there are many more people  who want to get out than people who  chose to stay some of the people who are  trying to get out hold us  um  permanent residence green cards  they cannot get out some of them do not  but they were our  translators  our guides our collaborators so we need  to do much more it's it's a bureaucratic  breakdown a mess  between the state department the defense  department there are a lot of volunteer  groups stepping in because the us  government is not doing a good job  saving the lives of people who risk  their lives  i have  friends who um know people who got  executed it is heartbreaking what  happened there  dr cohen and my final question to you  there's a lot that we've talked about  here and a lot that we didn't even get  to but we are limited in time it's a  hundred days into the fall of kabul what  do you see happening in the next three  months what does the situation on the  ground look like three months from now  when it's been six months total since  the taliban took over  you know there's a lot of chatter about  how taliban needs to become taliban  version 2.0 uh you know the nice and  fluffy taliban it's not going to happen  uh they're not going to abandon uh their  core beliefs their core values which are  the extremist  version of sharia law and start  cooperating with  us there are already hangings and  amputations for  relatively minor um infractions and  crimes um they are brutal and they are  not very important to understand they're  not a united body there are elements  that are terrorists like the haqqani  network that are in key positions in the  taliban government minister of interior  key ministers uh are  parts of the haqqani network which is  recognized as one of the worst terrorist  organizations so we need to work with  others in the region and for this uh  there's no choice by to talk to russia  china pakistan india and others and of  course our friends in central asia who  have better view  better contact with kabul and with the  parts of the taliban regime and we need  to increase our presence there and  around  afghanistan in terms of eyes and ears  and intelligence assets to understand  what the terrorist threats are going to  be  really appreciate you giving that  context for us 100 days in here dr ariel  cohen senior fellow with the atlantic  council thanks for joining us again here  on quick take that's going to do it for  us right now but stick with us for more  quick take ahead right here

